* empirical measurement is not sure
	* as example
		* we may measure wemen to be taller by men just by selecting men and wemen sample such that this proposition is true for that samples
	* hence it is not perfectly sure evidence
* still: by increasing the number of measurements [the number of samples]: empirical measurements can be sure enough for practical conclusions; thus empirical mesurement may be valid scientific method; but even only for that: they must adhere to some rigourous scientific requirements
* scientific requirements
	* for the samples to be highly representative of reality - the sample-set must contain high number of independent samples
	* science is verifiable knowledge, it does not need believing; hence a measurement method to be scientific it must by repeatable - an unambiguous discription of the method must exist, following of which other professionals would arrive at the same conclusion
* economics is highly connected with politics and hence is practiced not scientifically but fake-scientifically - omitting the scientific requirements but still pretending to be scientific
